// redun-deliv: redundant broadcast, LDFI (SIGMOD 2015) §3.1.3 and Figure 6. Every node that has a log entry "assumes
// responsibility for broadcasting it to all other nodes", at every step: redundancy in space and in time.
// Re-derived (derived = true). §3.1.3 adds `bcast(N, P)@next :- log(N, P);` to retry-deliv, which relays one step
// after the entry arrives; Figure 6's highlighted support `O(A,C,1) v O(C,B,2)` shows C relaying at the step it
// received the entry, and with the one-step delay the program has a counterexample at EOT 11 / EFF 10 / 1 crash,
// where Figure 13 certifies redun-deliv. The relay therefore reads `log` directly (same step), as Figure 6 draws it:
// Figure 2's send rule with the holder of a log entry in place of the broadcaster.
// Blossom golden corpus; see ../../README.md.

log(Node, Pload) :- bcast(Node, Pload);
node(Node, Neighbor)@next :- node(Node, Neighbor);
log(Node, Pload)@next :- log(Node, Pload);
log(Other, Pload)@async :- log(Holder, Pload), node(Holder, Other);
